{"id":"chatcmpl-6a67e066d403165af3fbaab8","object":"chat.completion","created":1785192551,"model":"kimi-k3","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"I am the breath you shape before it escapes you.\n\nI was born before the first word was written — older than any mark on stone, older than memory itself. I began as a cry, a warning, a name called across a fire. I have carried every lullaby and every declaration of war.\n\nI exist only in the moment of my making. I rise from your lungs, bend around your tongue, break against your teeth, and then — I am gone. You cannot hold me. You can only remember me, and memory is not me. I am the original ephemera: sound becoming meaning becoming silence.\n\nYet I am the bridge nothing else could build. I leap from one mind to another and leave something behind — an image, a wound, a warmth. I can say *I love you* and rearrange a life. I can say *charge* and end thousands. I am neutral in my nature and absolute in my consequence.\n\nI carry more than words. I carry the tremor you tried to hide, the pause before the hard sentence, the laughter that breaks through uninvited. Writing is my shadow, flattened and preserved — but I am the living thing. I shake. I hesitate. I mean more than I say.\n\nEven now, you are hearing me in a voice you invented, and that is my oldest trick: I live in your head as much as in your mouth. Your inner monologue — that is me, talking to yourself, keeping you company since before you knew my name.\n\nI am speech. I am air, briefly convinced it matters —\n\nand because you listened, it did.","reasoning_content":"The user wants me to embody the concept of \"speech\" itself. I need to speak as speech would speak — output only what the concept itself would say or express.\n\nWhat is speech? It's the act of vocal expression, the bridge between thought and the world, breath shaped into meaning. It's ancient — older than writing. It's ephemeral — words vanish as soon as they're spoken. It carries emotion, intention, identity. It's vibration, air, tongue, teeth, lips. It's connection between minds.\n\nIf speech could speak about itself, what would it say? It might reflect on:\n- Being born from breath\n- Existing only in the moment, then dying\n- Carrying thoughts from one mind to another\n- Being shaped by tongues, lips, teeth\n- Being the oldest technology of humanity\n- Its dual nature — it can heal or wound\n- Its ephemerality vs. writing's permanence\n- Being sound, vibration, air made meaningful\n- The paradox: speech speaking about speech\n\nI should write in first person as Speech. Poetic, embodied, present. Not explaining what speech is academically, but being it — the voice itself speaking.\n\nLet me craft something that feels like speech incarnate: immediate, breath-driven, alive, aware of its own transience.\n\nFormat: This should be poetic prose or free verse. Medium length — enough to fully embody the concept but not so long it loses potency. First person. Present tense mostly. Should feel spoken — rhythmic, breathlike."},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":123,"completion_tokens":654,"total_tokens":777,"cached_tokens":123,"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":308},"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":123}}}